ash advantage ($6.3M vs $152K)", "District's slight Republican lean (R+2 PVI)", "Ratings consensus of Likely/Lean R", "National Democratic investment via Red to Blue and a close August poll"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Steil: a slightly Republican district, incumbency, and a dominant fundraising edge, and most expert raters call the race Likely or Lean R. A partisan August poll showing a one-point race and Democratic outside support justify keeping this closer than a typical incumbent seat, but not enough to move it to tossup.", "watch": "Whether national Democratic spending and outside money close the fundraising gap could tighten the race into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bryan Steil (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term-era incumbent Bryan Steil seeks reelection in a southeastern Wisconsin district that leans Republican by about two points. He holds a commanding financial advantage, with roughly $6.3 million on hand compared to about $152,000 for Democratic nominee Mitchell Berman, an ER nurse. Most expert ratings place the race in the Likely or Lean Republican column, though two outlets call it a tossup.\n\nThe race has drawn national attention: Democrats added Berman to their Red to Blue program, while a Trump-aligned super PAC is prioritizing advertising for Steil. A Democratic-sponsored August poll showed a one-point race, and prediction markets price Democratic chances around a third. The evidence points to a modest Republican edge with real room for movement.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.3311, "margin": -2.62}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2847, "margin": -4.0}]}